From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Extortion Countermeasures in Crypto-Anarchy

Crypto-anarchy will greatly _reduce_ the scope of extortion
and the damage it causes.   

For extortion to work, the extorter has to know the 
victim's True Name, as well his physical location, rough estimate
of wealth, etc.  At the same time, the extorter has to either (a) remain 
anonymous or (b) demonstrate legitimacy via a reputation for "justice" and 
overwhelming physical force.  If neither (a) nor (b) be are met the extorter 
becomes vulnerable to blackmail and tit-for-tat extortion.  The very 
possibility of extortion depends on a large imbalance in either force,
privacy, or both.

And in fact this is true with our current dominant extortion rackets,
governments.  Extortion plays such an overwhelmingly important role
in our lives today that we have come to take it for granted.
States rely on a massive imbalance in information, 
on "crypto-gossip" in which information is gathered but hidden
from the unwashed masses.  Thus we have an uproar over Lotus
Marketplace as an "invasion of privacy", when in fact what it
did was break a crypto-gossip monopoly on private matters that
had been invaded long ago.  Thus we have Clipper where blackmail
is empowered by a monopoly on keys to private conversations, giving 
the extortion racket a free hand to blackmail its victims.  Numerous
other examples.  Extortion is the very basis of the state.  What 
crypto-anarchy does, first of all, is break up the monopoly on extortion:
the IRS is joined by a cacophony of other extorter wannabees.  Secondly, 
it incentives people to avoid extortion by making themselves 
"extortion-proof" -- which not coincidentally are the same exact steps 
needed needed to make onself "judgement proof".   Judgement-proofing 
destroys the capabilities of extorters both legitimate and noveau, 
bringing us to an era where extortion has become so expensive compared
to its benefits, that its scope will have been greatly reduced.

