Why should the members of a "crime organization" have less liability
than the actual criminal?

> Money laundering digital cash would be done by exchanging the dirty
> cash for clean cash--at a discount, of course.  The dirty cash is
> then reintroduced into the banking system after some number of
> anonymous, untraceable transactions.

Digital cash has to go back to the bank after every transaction.
Sorry, but I think that people here haven't read the protocols.

> This works because digital cash can be exchanged (and verified)
> without approval or knowledge of the authorities.  The first guy to
> deposit dirty cash into a government-controlled bank account can
> certainly be questioned, but how do you prove where he got the cash?

He had to have gotten it from the person who bought it. You can't take
digital cash and have it circulate like real dollar bills do. Anyone
who gets the cash can copy it, so the only way to know that the cash
is still good is to deposit it immediately, preferably on-line so you
can verify that the cash is good before the customer walks away.

